<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-inverses-as-system">
<title>The inversion problem as systems of equations</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case, carry out the following steps:
	<ol>

		<li>
			Compute the product matrix <m>A B</m>.
			(The result will be a matrix whose entries are formulas in the variables that make up the entries of <m>B</m>.)
		</li>

		<li>
			Use the definition of <term><xref ref="definition-matrix-ops-equal-matrices" text="title" /></term>
			to convert the matrix equality <m> A B = I </m> into equations in the variables that make up the entries of <m>B</m>
			(where, as usual, <m>I</m> represents the <xref ref="definition-inverses-identity-matrix" text="title" /> of appropriate dimensions).
		</li>

		<li>
			Solve the equations to compute the entries of <m>B</m>,
			which will then be equal to <m>\inv{A}</m>,
			or use the equations to decide that <m>A</m> has no inverse.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				3 \amp -8 \\
				2 \amp -5
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				a \amp b \\
				c \amp d
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m>,
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p><me>
			A B =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				3 \amp -8 \\
				2 \amp -5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{bmatrix}
				a \amp b \\
				c \amp d
			\end{bmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				3 a - 8 c \amp 3 b - 8 d \\
				2 a - 5 c \amp 2 b - 5 d
			\end{bmatrix}
		</me></p></li>

		<li><p>
			Matrix equality <m>A B = I</m> is
			<me>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					3 a - 8 c \amp 3 b - 8 d \\
					2 a - 5 c \amp 2 b - 5 d
				\end{bmatrix}
				= \bidentmattwo
			</me>.
			Equating corresponding entries leads to a pair of systems of equations:
			<md><mrow>
				\amp
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
					3 a \amp - \amp 8 c \amp = \amp 1 \\
					2 a \amp - \amp 5 c \amp = \amp 0
				\end{sysofeqns}
				\text{,}
				\amp
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
					3 b \amp - \amp 8 d \amp = \amp 0 \\
					2 b \amp - \amp 5 d \amp = \amp 1
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</mrow></md>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Put each of the systems of equations into an augmented matrices and reduce to solve.
			(Notice how <m>A</m> ends up being the coefficient matrix of both systems,
			and the column of constants is one of the columns of the identity matrix!)
			First,
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
					3 \amp -8 \amp 1 \\
					2 \amp -5 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
					1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp -2
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>,
			from which we obtain <m>a = -5</m>, <m>c = -2</m>.
			Next,
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
					3 \amp -8 \amp 0 \\
					2 \amp -5 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr|r}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 8 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 3
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>,
			from which we obtain <m>b = 8</m>, <m>d = 3</m>.
			Therefore, we have
			<me>
				\inv{A} = B =
				\begin{bmatrix}
					-5 \amp 8 \\
					-2 \amp 3
				\end{bmatrix}
			</me>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				6 \amp -5 \amp -6 \\
				0 \amp -3 \amp -6 \\
				6 \amp -2 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				a \amp b \amp c \\
				d \amp e \amp f \\
				g \amp h \amp i
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m>,
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p><md>
			<mrow>
				A B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					6 \amp -5 \amp -6 \\
					0 \amp -3 \amp -6 \\
					6 \amp -2 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{bmatrix}
					a \amp b \amp c \\
					d \amp e \amp f \\
					g \amp h \amp i
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{bmatrix}
					6 a - 5 d - 6 g \amp  6 b - 5 e - 6 h \amp  6 c - 5 f - 6 i \\
					   -3 d - 6 g   \amp    -3 e - 6 h    \amp    -3 f - 6 i    \\
					    6 a - 2 d   \amp     6 b - 2 e    \amp     6 c - 2 f
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>
		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			Matrix equality <m>A B = I</m> is
			<me>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					6 a - 5 d - 6 g \amp  6 b - 5 e - 6 h \amp  6 c - 5 f - 6 i \\
					   -3 d - 6 g   \amp    -3 e - 6 h    \amp    -3 f - 6 i    \\
					    6 a - 2 d   \amp     6 b - 2 e    \amp     6 c - 2 f
				\end{bmatrix}
				= \bidentmatthree
			</me>.
			Equating corresponding entries leads to a triple of systems of equations:
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					6 a \amp - \amp  5 d \amp - \amp 6 g \amp = \amp 1 \\
					    \amp   \amp -3 d \amp - \amp 6 g \amp = \amp 0 \\
					6 a \amp - \amp  2 d \amp   \amp     \amp = \amp 0
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>,
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					6 b \amp - \amp  5 e \amp - \amp 6 h \amp = \amp 0 \\
					    \amp   \amp -3 e \amp - \amp 6 h \amp = \amp 1 \\
					6 b \amp - \amp  2 e \amp   \amp     \amp = \amp 0
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>,
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					6 c \amp - \amp  5 f \amp - \amp 6 i \amp = \amp 0 \\
					    \amp   \amp -3 f \amp - \amp 6 i \amp = \amp 0 \\
					6 c \amp - \amp  2 f \amp   \amp     \amp = \amp 1
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Let's put the first system of equations into an augmented matrices and reduce to solve.
			(Notice how <m>A</m> ends up being the coefficient matrix,
			and the column of constants is the first column of the identity matrix!)
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
					6 \amp -5 \amp -6 \amp 1 \\
					0 \amp -3 \amp -6 \amp 0 \\
					6 \amp -2 \amp  0 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{ccc|c}
					1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2}{3} \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp      2      \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp      0      \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>
			This RREF matrix has a leading one in the <q>equals</q> column,
			which indicates that the system is inconsistent.
			It doesn't matter whether the other two systems are consistent or not,
			because the inconsistency of this first system means it is impossible to determine the values of entries <m>a,d,g</m> in <m>B</m> so that <m>A B = I</m> is true.
			Therefore, <m>A</m> is not invertible.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-as-system-3x3-invertible">

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
				 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
				-1 \amp 1 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				a \amp b \amp c \\
				d \amp e \amp f \\
				g \amp h \amp i
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m>,
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p><md>
			<mrow>
				A B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
					 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
					-1 \amp 1 \amp -2
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{bmatrix}
					a \amp b \amp c \\
					d \amp e \amp f \\
					g \amp h \amp i
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 a + d - 3 g \amp  b + e - 3 h \amp  c + f - 3 i \\
					   d - 3 g   \amp    e - 3 h   \amp    f - 3 i   \\
					-a + d - 2 g \amp -b + e - 2 h \amp -c + f - 2 i
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>
		</md></p></li>

		<li><p>
			Matrix equality <m>A B = I</m> is
			<me>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					 a + d - 3 g \amp  b + e - 3 h \amp  c + f - 3 i \\
					   d - 3 g   \amp    e - 3 h   \amp    f - 3 i   \\
					-a + d - 2 g \amp -b + e - 2 h \amp -c + f - 2 i
				\end{bmatrix}
				= \bidentmatthree
			</me>.
			Equating corresponding entries leads to a triple of systems of equations:
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					 a \amp + \amp d \amp - \amp 3 g \amp = \amp 1 \\
					   \amp   \amp d \amp - \amp 3 g \amp = \amp 0 \\
					-a \amp + \amp d \amp - \amp 2 g \amp = \amp 0
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>,
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					 b \amp + \amp e \amp - \amp 3 h \amp = \amp 0 \\
					   \amp   \amp e \amp - \amp 3 h \amp = \amp 1 \\
					-b \amp + \amp e \amp - \amp 2 h \amp = \amp 0
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>,
			<me>
				\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
					 c \amp + \amp f \amp - \amp 3 i \amp = \amp 0 \\
					   \amp   \amp f \amp - \amp 3 i \amp = \amp 0 \\
					-c \amp + \amp f \amp - \amp 2 i \amp = \amp 1
				\end{sysofeqns}
			</me>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Put each of the systems of equations into an augmented matrices and reduce to solve.
			(Notice how <m>A</m> ends up being the coefficient matrix of both systems,
			and the column of constants is one of the columns of the identity matrix!)
			First,
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
					 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 1 \\
					 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
					-1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{ccc|c}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>,
			from which we obtain <m>a = 1</m>, <m>d = 3</m>, <m>g = 1</m>.
			Next,
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
					 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
					 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 1 \\
					-1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{ccc|r}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>,
			from which we obtain <m>b = -1</m>, <m>e = -5</m>, <m>h = -2</m>.
			<me>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
					 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
					 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
					-1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\quad \rowredarrow \quad
				\begin{abmatrix}{ccc|r}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>,
			from which we obtain <m>c = 0</m>, <m>f = 3</m>, <m>i = 1</m>.
			Therefore, we have
			<me>
				\inv{A} = B =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
					3 \amp -5 \amp 3 \\
					1 \amp -2 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</me>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
